  "query_preview": "..The primary role for a meteorologist is to spend\ncountless hours investigating what the atmosphere will throw our\nway next, carefully determining its potential negative impacts,\nthen crafting a method to express those thoughts and concerns to\nall those who may be impacted. Sometimes, it is easy to overlook\nthat the best forecast may actually be one that encourages \neveryone to go outside, look-up, take in the scenery, and \nappreciate the beauty of Southeast Arizona and the weather that \nallows it to breathe.\n\nThe next few days will be gorgeous...which I would guess is no \nsurprise given it is late October. Skies sunny to mostly sunny\nand afternoon temperatures generally be in the 80s...ranging \nfrom 3 to 6 degrees below normal today, warming to a few degrees\nabove normal the first half of the upcoming week. Enjoy it,\nappreciate it.\n\nThe upper pattern today is defined by an upper-low over the\nnorthern half of Arizona and Utah, an upper low near the British\nColumbia coastline, and a strong upper-low in the Eastern \nPacific near 33N/148W. The upper-low over AZ/UT will eject NE \ntoday/Monday as the trough in the Pacific Northwest amplifies\nand progresses inland across Washington and Oregon. Strong flow\ndeveloping on the backside of this feature will first reinforce\nthe Pacific Northwest trough, then produce a mean trough that \nhelps weaken the closed circulation over the Eastern pacific and\neventually allows this system to open into a progressive\ntrough during the latter half of the week. This feature will \nweaken as it moves east, but southerly flow developing ahead of\nthe trough should encourage a northward surge of tropical\nmoisture, some of which may be associated with Hurricane Vance,\nthrough the Gulf of California and eventually into southwest  \nArizona Thursday and Friday. The 15/12Z GEFS was bullish with \nthe moisture, with precipitable water values 2.0 to 2.5 standard\ndeviations above climatological normal. This should result in\nscattered showers and thunderstorms for Southeast Arizona as\nearly as Thursday night, but more likely Friday.\n\nElsewhere, additional shortwave energy reinforcing the mean trough\nover the Eastern Pacific late Friday into Saturday will close \noff another low near 30N/130W by 01/00Z Saturday afternoon\nbefore shifting east toward Arizona Sunday. This low will tap \ninto a deep plume of subtropical moisture as it makes its way \ninto Arizona with GEFS indicating PWATs in this plume 2.5 to 3.0\nstandard deviations above normal. Strong SWLY flow will likely \nfocus the heaviest precipitation into the Mogollon Rim with the\norographic lift, but the combination of anomalous moisture and \nincreasing large-scale ascent may also support periods of heavy\nprecipitation across Southeast Arizona Sunday into Monday. This\nis supported by the 25/13Z NBM, with a 30-50 percent probability\nof 48-hour pcpn amounts in excess of an inch Sunday and Monday,\nand even a 10-30% of exceeding 2 inches, particularly from \nTucson northward.\n\nAt this time, it looks like Halloween may be the lull in storm\nactivity in between these two distinct weather systems Friday \nand Sunday...but timing can change, and residual moisture and\ngeneralized instability may result in a low-end chance of showers\nnone-the-less.",
